--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHY v3.1 EXISTS -- THE REGISTER OVERFLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  v3.0 died on load in modern executors with:
      "Out of local registers when trying to allocate def: exceeded limit 200"
  Root cause: Luau caps EVERY function scope at 200 local registers, and the
  main chunk is a function like any other. v3.0 declared ~207 top-level locals
  (12 services, ~60 state scalars, ~100 utility/feature functions, 8 UI tab
  handles, 8 compat wrappers). Register #201 landed around the UI API-detection
  section (executor line ~2661) and the compiler refused the whole chunk.

  v3.1 restructures the same logic into register-safe containers:
    CFG   -- constants (ids, caps, color table, part-name whitelist)
    S     -- all mutable runtime state (every scalar that was a top-level local)
    Antis -- every start/stop feature function, one namespace, one register
    UI    -- Rayfield tab handles + wrapper access
    Local tables kept top-level (hot, referenced everywhere):
      Variables, stats, ICONS, WhitelistedSKS, KeyBinds, mapSaves,
      loadedMapGroups, loopThreads, Connections
  One-shot setup code lives inside do ... end blocks so its registers are
  released when the block closes. Top-level local count is now ~98 of 200,
  leaving headroom for future commands and UI elements.

